clearcote(#12): a persona window reports its REAL frame delta.

outerHeight() synthesised the browser chrome as a hard-coded innerHeight + 88
whenever a persona was active, clamped to the persona work area. Pointer events,
meanwhile, carry the true viewport origin, shifted together with window.screenY
by PersonaScreenOriginDelta so those two remain consistent with each other.

That left a page two ways to recover the chrome height -- from an event, as
screenY - clientY - window.screenY, and from the window, as outerHeight -
innerHeight -- which disagreed by however far the real chrome was from 88.
Measured on a headed Windows desktop with a real frame delta of 129: the two
readings differed by 41px, a contradiction no window can produce, and one the
public audit reconciles directly (event-coords-vs-window-geometry).

Use the real delta instead. This leaks nothing further: innerHeight is already
the true viewport, and the chrome height is a constant of the OS and its display
scaling rather than a property of the display size or the window position. The
clamp to the persona work area is kept, so a window still never claims to be
taller than the screen it says it is on.

Also removes a misplaced block: the avail_width / persona WIDTH branches sat
inside outerHeight(), so with --fingerprint-avail-width set and no
--fingerprint-avail-height, outerHeight() returned a value derived from
innerWidth. The second of the two was unreachable in any case, since the persona
branch above it always returned first. outerWidth() is unaffected and continues
to report the real frame width.

--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/core/frame/local_dom_window.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/core/frame/local_dom_window.cc
@@ -203,6 +203,22 @@
   return document->RequestAnimationFrame(frame_callback);
 }
 
+
+// clearcote(#12): the REAL outer-minus-inner vertical delta for this window --
+// the height of the browser chrome plus the bottom border. Used so a persona
+// reports a frame the pointer-event coordinates can be reconciled with. Returns
+// 0 rather than a negative when the frame cannot be measured, which keeps
+// outerHeight >= innerHeight in every degenerate case.
+int RealFrameDeltaHeightFor(LocalFrame* frame, int inner_height) {
+  if (!frame)
+    return 0;
+  Page* page = frame->GetPage();
+  if (!page)
+    return 0;
+  const int real = page->GetChromeClient().RootWindowRect(*frame).height();
+  const int delta = real - inner_height;
+  return delta > 0 ? delta : 0;
+}
 }  // namespace
 
